opriate escalation path is cross-scale material design, not blind architectural iteration. 36	 37	## Material system and mapping anchor 38	 39	Selected baseline parameter set: `Chen2020`. 40	 41	Deterministic mapping basis: 42	 43	- Task text has no explicit electrode system. 44	- Protocol rule: no explicit system stated → default to `Chen2020` with an explicit record. 45	 46	Operational consequence: 47	 48	- Architecture exploration is the primary first-round lever. 49	- Electrolyte transport (`D_e`, `sigma_e`, `t_+`) and thermal coefficient `h` are unlockable via the widest boundary default in this case, because the task text does not lock them. 50	 51	## Degree-of-freedom boundary (parsed from task text + headless defaults) 52	 53	1. Electrode system   54	   - Adjustable: yes.   55	   - First-round choice: locked to `Chen2020` as the baseline system for initial architecture characterization.   56	   - Escalation rule: if baseline architecture+formulation space still cannot reach the target, propose alternate systems/additives at Stage 2. 57	 58	2. Electrolyte formulation   59	   - Adjustable: yes.   60	   - Interpretation: widest allowed by task text, because no constraint was stated.   61	   - Parameters eligible for override: `Electrolyte diffusivity [m2.s-1]`, `Electrolyte conductivity [S.m-1]`, `Cation transference number`. 62	 63	3. Electrode modification   64	   - Adjustable: yes, if needed in later rounds.   65	   - First-round priority: lower than architecture and transport, because no coating/dopant was named. 66	 67	4. Cell architecture   68	   - Adjustable: yes.   69	   - Explicitly explorable parameters: electrode thicknesses, porosities, N/P ratio, separator thickness and porosity, current collector thicknesses, particle radii. 70	 71	5. Thermal management   72	   - Adjustable: yes.   73	   - Parameter eligible for override: `Total heat transfer coefficient`. 74	 75	Baseline-default interpretation for parameters not overridden in a round: 76	 77	- Electrolyte uses default transport parameters unless explicitly overridden. 78	- Architecture uses baseline thickness/porosity/particle-size defaults unless explicitly overridden. 79	- Thermal management uses baseline `h` unless explicitly overridden. 80	 81	## Ablation switches 82	 83	Defaults used for this headless case, unless overridden later: 84	 85	- `exploration_force`: ON   86	- `ceiling_escalation`: ON   87	- `funnel_voting`: ON 88	 89	These states are recorded because the task text declares no ablation mode. 90	 91	## Candidate strategy 92	 93	### Round 1 plan 94	 95	1. Run baseline `Chen2020` 1C discharge to establish the anchor operating point (`capacity_ah`, `energy_density_wh_kg`). 96	2. Use `calc-energy` to compute contract-grade gravimetric energy density and supporting mass/thickness metrics. 97	3. Run at least two architecture variants in the same round: 98	   - Variant A: thinner electrodes / higher rate-friendly transport path. 99	   - Variant B: thinner current collectors / higher active-material fraction. 100	4. Begin coarse thermal/plating assessment on the baseline system if architecture looks promising. 101	 102	### Fallback routing (applied after each evaluation) 103	 104	- If ED is too low but 4C safety looks favorable → return to Stage 3 and push architecture toward higher energy density. 105	- If ED looks feasible but 4C plating occurs → return to Stage 3 and improve transport/thermal boundary conditions (porosity, particle size, electrolyte transport, `h`). 106	- If architecture + formulation space is exhausted and ED ceiling remains below target → escalate to Stage 2 material design / system switching. 107	 108	## Budget allocation (conceptual, not a hard round cap) 109	 110	- First 3 rounds: baseline and coarse architecture exploration. 111	- Next 2–3 rounds: coupled 4C safety screening and temperature/plating mitigation. 112	- Additional rounds if needed: transport-tuned Pareto iterations or material escalation. 113	 114	## Risk and fallback plan 115	 116	1. **Primary risk:** ED infeasibility under `Chen2020` defaults.   117	   - First response: aggressive but physically plausible architecture tuning plus transport improvement. 118	   - If still infeasible: escalate into material/system design (Stage 2). 119	 120	2. **Primary fast-charge risk:** lithium plating.   121	   - First response: increase porosity, reduce particle radius, and improve electrolyte transport parameters. 122	   - If ED too low afterward: re-balance ED vs rate capability. 123	 124	3. **Primary thermal risk:** failure to stay ≤ 333.15 K during 4C charge.   125	   - First response: transport/diffusivity improvements and increased `h`. 126	   - Risk: excessive thermal improvements may be unrealistic if not supported by plausible formulation/system changes. 127	 128	4. **Logging risk:** headless mode may incorrectly restrict allowed design levers if freedom boundaries are set too narrowly.   129	   - Mitigation: this plan adopts the widest allowed interpretation for unspecified degrees of freedom. 130	 131	## References (domain basis for strategy) 132	 133	- `Chen2020` parameter set usage → base-parameter anchor table in VBF protocol (`SKILL.md` §1.5). 134	- Architecture/transport/mitigation mapping → VBF protocol and CLI-command reference in `.claude/skills/virtual-battery-factory`. 135	- Widest interpretation of unspecified freedoms → VBF headless-execution rule and lessons recorded in protocol. 136	- Plating/treatment of safety metrics → VBF protocol Stage 4 and evaluation guidance. 137	- Practical direction only: high-energy dense cells typically require either electrode thinning, inert-mass reduction, improved electrolyte transport, or higher-voltage/high-capacity active materials. 138
