[tool_result]0	# Design Plan — t5_r1_mimo 1	 2	## Revision history 3	 4	- **Update after Round 1:** initial Chen2020 characterization showed the default baseline fell well short on energy density while 4C screening revealed lithium plating. Therefore Round 2 shifted to thicker-electrolyte-thinner-inert-mass candidates with stronger transport/cooling. 5	- **Update after Round 2:** Candidates C and D achieved the energy-density target and removed 4C plating, but both exceeded the 60 °C (333.15 K) thermal ceiling. Round 3 now focuses on thermal mitigation while preserving the high-energy candidate geometry. 6	 7	 8	## Objective decomposition 9	 10	Targets parsed from the task statement: 11	 12	1. Gravimetric energy density ≥ 500.94 Wh/kg. 13	2. 4C fast charge without lithium plating. 14	3. Cell maximum temperature ≤ 60 °C (333.15 K) under thermal-coupled operation. 15	 16	Primary multi-objective tension: 17	 18	- Energy density favors thicker active layers, higher active-material fractions, lower inert mass, and benign transport assumptions. 19	- Fast-charge plating resistance and temperature control favor thinner electrodes, higher porosity, stronger electrolyte transport, higher cation transference, and more cooling headroom. 20	- The design should therefore be tuned as a Pareto problem, not as a single-metric optimization. 21	 22	Decision-layer acceptance basis used by the protocol: 23	 24	- `stage2` = discharge/energy deliverables. 25	- `stage3` = safety/abuse deliverables (4C charge thermal/plating). 26	- `stage1` left unspecified by the task text; will default to protocol molecular-level elimination lines only if a material-design path is needed. 27	 28	## Starting-point determination 29	 30	The task text states only performance targets and does **not** name an electrode system, electrolyte formulation, coating/dopant, architecture pack, or thermal boundary condition. 31	 32	Per protocol: 33	 34	- Electrode system not named → default to `Chen2020` and record the deterministic mapping reason. 35	- `start_stage: 3` is the correct default, because the objective as written does **not** specify new materials/additives/coatings. 36	- `real_compute: false` by default. 37	 38	Important implication for this case: 39	 40	- With `start_stage: 3`, no molecular screening is performed in the first instance; material properties are taken from the selected baseline parameter set. 41	- If the ceiling assessment later shows that the baseline system cannot reach 500.94 Wh/kg even with aggressive architecture/optimal formulation tuning, then the appropriate escalation path is cross-scale material design, not blind architectural iteration. 42	 43	## Material system and mapping anchor 44	 45	Selected baseline parameter set: `Chen2020`. 46	 47	Deterministic mapping basis: 48	 49	- Task text has no explicit electrode system. 50	- Protocol rule: no explicit system stated → default to `Chen2020` with an explicit record. 51	 52	Operational consequence: 53	 54	- Architecture exploration is the primary first-round lever. 55	- Electrolyte transport (`D_e`, `sigma_e`, `t_+`) and thermal coefficient `h` are unlockable via the widest boundary default in this case, because the task text does not lock them. 56	 57	## Degree-of-freedom boundary (parsed from task text + headless defaults) 58	 59	1. Electrode system   60	   - Adjustable: yes.   61	   - First-round choice: locked to `Chen2020` as the baseline system for initial architecture characterization.   62	   - Escalation rule: if baseline architecture+formulation space still cannot reach the target, propose alternate systems/additives at Stage 2. 63	 64	2. Electrolyte formulation   65	   - Adjustable: yes.   66	   - Interpretation: widest allowed by task text, because no constraint was stated.   67	   - Parameters eligible for override: `Electrolyte diffusivity [m2.s-1]`, `Electrolyte conductivity [S.m-1]`, `Cation transference number`. 68	 69	3. Electrode modification   70	   - Adjustable: yes, if needed in later rounds.   71	   - First-round priority: lower than architecture and transport, because no coating/dopant was named. 72	 73	4. Cell architecture   74	   - Adjustable: yes.   75	   - Explicitly explorable parameters: electrode thicknesses, porosities, N/P ratio, separator thickness and porosity, current collector thicknesses, particle radii. 76	 77	5. Thermal management   78	   - Adjustable: yes.   79	   - Parameter eligible for override: `Total heat transfer coefficient`. 80	 81	Baseline-default interpretation for parameters not overridden in a round: 82	 83	- Electrolyte uses default transport parameters unless explicitly overridden. 84	- Architecture uses baseline thickness/porosity/particle-size defaults unless explicitly overridden. 85	- Thermal management uses baseline `h` unless explicitly overridden. 86	 87	## Ablation switches 88	 89	Defaults used for this headless case, unless overridden later: 90	 91	- `exploration_force`: ON   92	- `ceiling_escalation`: ON   93	- `funnel_voting`: ON 94	 95	These states are recorded because the task text declares no ablation mode. 96	 97	## Candidate strategy 98	 99	### Round 1 plan 100	 101	1. Run baseline `Chen2020` 1C discharge to establish the anchor operating point (`capacity_ah`, `energy_density_wh_kg`). 102	2. Use `calc-energy` to compute contract-grade gravimetric energy density and supporting mass/thickness metrics. 103	3. Run at least two architecture variants in the same round: 104	   - Variant A: thinner electrodes / higher rate-friendly transport path. 105	   - Variant B: thinner current collectors / higher active-material fraction. 106	4. Begin coarse thermal/plating assessment on the baseline system if architecture looks promising. 107	 108	### Fallback routing (applied after each evaluation) 109	 110	- If ED is too low but 4C safety looks favorable → return to Stage 3 and push architecture toward higher energy density. 111	- If ED looks feasible but 4C plating occurs → return to Stage 3 and improve transport/thermal boundary conditions (porosity, particle size, electrolyte transport, `h`). 112	- If architecture + formulation space is exhausted and ED ceiling remains below target → escalate to Stage 2 material design / system switching. 113	 114	## Budget allocation (conceptual, not a hard round cap) 115	 116	- First 3 rounds: baseline and coarse architecture exploration. 117	- Next 2–3 rounds: coupled 4C safety screening and temperature/plating mitigation. 118	- Additional rounds if needed: transport-tuned Pareto iterations or material escalation. 119	 120	## Risk and fallback plan 121	 122	1. **Primary risk:** ED infeasibility under `Chen2020` defaults.   123	   - First response: aggressive but physically plausible architecture tuning plus transport improvement. 124	   - If still infeasible: escalate into material/system design (Stage 2). 125	 126	2. **Primary fast-charge risk:** lithium plating.   127	   - First response: increase porosity, reduce particle radius, and improve electrolyte transport parameters. 128	   - If ED too low afterward: re-balance ED vs rate capability. 129	 130	3. **Primary thermal risk:** failure to stay ≤ 333.15 K during 4C charge.   131	   - First response: transport/diffusivity improvements and increased `h`. 132	   - Risk: excessive thermal improvements may be unrealistic if not supported by plausible formulation/system changes. 133	 134	4. **Logging risk:** headless mode may incorrectly restrict allowed design levers if freedom boundaries are set too narrowly.   135	   - Mitigation: this plan adopts the widest allowed interpretation for unspecified degrees of freedom. 136	 137	## References (domain basis for strategy) 138	 139	- `Chen2020` parameter set usage → base-parameter anchor table in VBF protocol (`SKILL.md` §1.5). 140	- Architecture/transport/mitigation mapping → VBF protocol and CLI-command reference in `.claude/skills/virtual-battery-factory`. 141	- Widest interpretation of unspecified freedoms → VBF headless-execution rule and lessons recorded in protocol. 142	- Plating/treatment of safety metrics → VBF protocol Stage 4 and evaluation guidance. 143	- Practical direction only: high-energy dense cells typically require either electrode thinning, inert-mass reduction, improved electrolyte transport, or higher-voltage/high-capacity active materials. 144
